WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:04.370
- I think the stat is 78%
of athletes go bankrupt

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.270
within two years, NFL players
go bankrupt within two years

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.130
after they retire.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.990
And you think to yourself,
like you made all this money,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.270
how did you not be smarter with it?

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:12.540
Well, they never taught us in school,

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.299
how to manage our finances

00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:16.730
and how to save money
and how to invest money.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.170
They don't teach you these
things when all you care

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:19.830
about is sports.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.090
So I didn't make that much
money playing arena football.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.720
I made $250 a week and we had food stamps.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.960
So I was living in Huntsville, Alabama,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.710
and eating Chick-fil-A and
whatever else I could eat from

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:31.653
the food stamps.

00:00:32.731 --> 00:00:34.160
And so there was no money that I saved.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.419
My sister brought me in and
let me crash at her house,

00:00:37.419 --> 00:00:38.610
it was supposed to be for about a month.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.250
A month turned into a year and a half

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.180
and she gave me an incredible gift.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.920
She said, "Okay, you
gotta start paying rent,"

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.575
after a year and a half.

00:00:46.575 --> 00:00:49.242
(dynamic music)

00:00:55.915 --> 00:00:58.309
- This is the Rubin report,
and I'm still Dave Rubin.

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:00.280
Friendly reminder guys to
subscribe to our YouTube channel

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.570
and click that pesky notification bell

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.640
so that you might just see our new videos

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:05.770
when they come out.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.700
And joining me today is a New York Times

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.920
bestselling author, a business coach,

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.310
and the host of the School
of Greatness Podcast,

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:14.810
Lewis Howes, welcome to the Rubin report.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.400
- My man, thanks for having me.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.640
- I'm glad to have you here.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.810
We got to get some simple
stuff out of the way

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:21.643
before we do anything.

00:01:21.643 --> 00:01:23.730
First off, I love that freaking jacket.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:24.563
- I know.
- I almost bought

00:01:24.563 --> 00:01:26.243
that jacket--
- It's amazing.

00:01:26.243 --> 00:01:27.410
- I'm jealous about the jacket.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:30.685
That's like Han Solo modern, I love it.

00:01:30.685 --> 00:01:31.750
- It's clean, right?
- It's clean,

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.310
I want that jacket.
- Just a little touch

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.160
of like, the accents are super clean.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.200
- You put anything in there?

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.120
- I don't, no.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.456
I should.

00:01:38.456 --> 00:01:39.289
I've only worn this a few times,

00:01:39.289 --> 00:01:40.130
so I should probably wear it more.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:41.733
I put this on for you.

00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:43.061
I knew you were gonna wear a jacket

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:44.640
and I normally just wear a
black shirt, but I was like,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.490
let me wear something a little bit nicer.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.640
We're an actual professional studio here.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.473
I had to dress up.

00:01:49.473 --> 00:01:52.090
- Well, you got a pretty
professional operation too.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.390
I did your podcast in the
midst of the lockdown.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.120
It's the only live in-person interview

00:01:58.120 --> 00:01:59.200
that I did in the whole thing.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.510
We actually--
- We shook hands.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.040
- Well, we fist bumped
and then we realized,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.657
let's just do it.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:02:03.657 --> 00:02:04.801
- And we actually shook hands.

00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:06.050
- And then we found out right afterwards

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.700
that you hit the New York
Times bestseller list

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.780
for your big book, so.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.680
- It was in your studio that I found out.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.330
- Magic happens with us.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.640
- Two New York Times bestselling authors.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.870
There we go, there we go.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.507
All right, there's a ton I
want to talk to you about,

00:02:19.507 --> 00:02:21.430
and I'm glad we're doing
this because you know,

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.860
I do a lot of politics in here.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.200
I do a lot of culture wars and all that,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.340
but I think underneath all
of that is a human thing

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.300
that I think you are really
excelling at at the moment.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.640
So before we get into that, though,

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.520
for the people that have
no idea who you are,

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.940
who is Lewis Howes?

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.730
How the hell did he get that fancy jacket,

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:40.870
and what's he doing here?

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.910
- I'm a human being on a quest
to discover more of myself,

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.360
how I can become better and
how I can make a big impact

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.810
on the people around me.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.690
And I've always cared
about human performance.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.690
I've always just wanted to excel
at the things I care about.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:56.970
I think from an early age,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.680
I did it to try to prove people wrong

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.160
because I felt unseen, unheard, un-liked,

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.480
unloved by everyone around me.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.720
And even though that wasn't the truth,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:07.970
that's just how I felt.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.160
My parents were there for me.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.340
My older siblings were there,

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.940
but I just didn't feel like
I fit in in a lot of ways.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.390
And especially in school,
I was very dyslexic.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:18.650
If I even try to read my own book

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.730
I'd probably struggle reading my own words

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.540
that I wrote down, just
because I've always

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:24.373
struggled with that.

00:03:24.373 --> 00:03:26.553
And I was in special needs classes

00:03:26.553 --> 00:03:28.310
from as long as I can remember.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:31.090
During recess and lunch breaks in school,

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.580
I would be with tutors.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:33.990
So I didn't get to do a lot of the things

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.697
that normal kids would do.

00:03:35.697 --> 00:03:37.810
And I just felt always insecure

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:41.050
and insignificant in my abilities.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:42.750
And that kind of stayed with me.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.940
And I use sports as my afterschool
kind of program to say,

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.340
what can I do if school is
something I struggle in,

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.580
where in high school, they would,

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:52.750
I don't know if they did
this in your high school,

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.620
but they would actually
rank us in our class

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.010
on our report cards.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.070
So I was always in the bottom four

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.580
of the student class.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:01.680
- Like, purely on grade?

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.860
- They would like say like if
there're a hundred students,

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.170
I was like always 96 out of a hundred

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.300
or 100 out of a hundred based on your GPA.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.820
So I would always see that number

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:11.810
and dread it every quarter,

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.200
we would get our report card,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.690
and I'd always see I'm on the bottom four.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:17.470
I never broke out of the
bottom four in school.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:20.970
So it was just a very
challenging time for me to,

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.318
to think about it.

00:04:22.318 --> 00:04:23.850
And that's what we judged
ourselves on it in school growing

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.580
up was like, who can read faster?

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:28.100
Who's smarter, who got
A's and who got D's?

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.550
And so I use sports as my
outlet to say, I'm, you know,

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:32.820
I'm gonna become bigger, faster,

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.410
and stronger than everyone else around me.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.950
I'm gonna show them that I'm
worthy of love and worthy

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.680
of their attention, I'm
worthy of being picked first,

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.940
not being last.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.550
And I just put my energy into
that and made my dreams around

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.590
that because I started to
get really good at sports.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:51.640
And that became kind of my
mission until I got injured,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.610
playing football.
- Then sports ends.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:54.850
And then that--
- Then my dream,

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.510
my identity, you know, kind of falls apart

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.030
because I got injured
playing arena football.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.570
And I had, I mean, this is
in 2008 when the economy,

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:06.220
it was the end of 2007,
the economy is crashing.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.639
I remember I didn't have
a college degree yet,

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:09.830
because I left early to go play football,

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:10.730
try to make the NFL.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:12.840
Wasn't good enough to make it.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.580
And I had no money.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.140
I had college debt that
I was trying to pay off.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.223
I didn't have a job.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:22.880
And I remember trying to go on LinkedIn

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.130
to find jobs in 2007, 2008.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:26.340
And no one was hiring people

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.300
without a college degree in 2008,

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:29.670
let alone with master's degrees.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.890
They were having a hard
time getting a job.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:33.890
So I just remember saying to
myself, what am I gonna do?

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.070
I'm sleeping on my sister's
couch at this time for a year

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.960
and a half, trying to
figure out one, who am I?

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.080
I lost my dream.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.380
I lost the ability to
work out at this moment.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:46.760
Everything I put my
identity around to is gone.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:48.340
I have no clue what to do next.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.610
And this might be a first world problem.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.040
But for me, my identity was gone

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.290
and I didn't know who I was in the world

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.560
or why I was even supposed
to be here anymore.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.390
- How dark was that time?

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.780
Because you see, you hear this
with athletes all the time,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.310
major pro guys that you
know, they blow out the knee

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.481
and it's over, and then, you know,

00:06:05.481 --> 00:06:06.660
you find out that they're
living on the streets

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.620
five years later, all sorts--

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:12.221
- I think the stat is 78%
of athletes go bankrupt

00:06:12.221 --> 00:06:15.290
within two years, NFL players go bankrupt

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:16.700
within two years after they retire.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.570
And you think to yourself,
like you made all this money,

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:19.860
how did you not be smarter with it?

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.120
Well, they never taught us in school,

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:23.210
how to manage our finances

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.310
and how to save money and
how to even invest money.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:26.740
They don't teach you these
things when all you care

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.410
about is sports.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:30.670
So I didn't make that much
money playing arena football.

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:33.290
I made $250 a week and we had food stamps.

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:35.550
So I was living in Huntsville, Alabama,

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.510
and eating Chick-fil-A and whatever else

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.870
I could eat from the food stamps.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:42.740
And so there was no money that I saved.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.610
My sister brought me in and
let me crash at her house.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:47.050
It was supposed to be for about a month.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:48.840
A month turned into a year and a half.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.760
And she gave me an incredible gift.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:53.500
She said, "Okay, you
gotta start paying rent,"

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:54.780
after a year and a half.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:59.117
So that push finally got me
to say, "Okay, I need to learn

00:06:59.117 --> 00:07:01.630
"if I'm not gonna go get a job somewhere."

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:04.360
I started truck driving for $250 a week.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:08.480
I was driving Napa car auto
parts for six hours a day.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.630
- 250 was your sweet spot.
- 250 was my sweet spot.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:11.860
I couldn't break that limit.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:13.910
So I was like, "How do I get past this?"

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:15.140
And at the same time,

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:16.950
I was also reaching out
to people on LinkedIn,

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.940
finding, trying to find mentors.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:21.220
I was just trying to
interview people personally

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:22.300
and ask them questions about

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.860
how do I achieve anything after sports.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.890
I just had no clue how to do it.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:28.130
And she gave me a great gift.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.817
She said, "You've got to start helping

00:07:29.817 --> 00:07:32.020
"and contributing to the rent."

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:35.390
And I didn't know what I was gonna do.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:36.750
I really just like, "I don't
know what I'm gonna do,"

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.690
but I applied for a job on LinkedIn.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:42.480
I found a sports marketing job
'cause I eventually graduated

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.760
and applied for a sports marketing job.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.430
And I got an interview.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.400
My first ever interview that
I got when applying for a job.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:50.590
And for whatever reason,

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:53.430
I had my one nice sport
jacket from like college.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:57.550
I dressed up and I'm leaving
the house, my sister's house.

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:00.730
I open the door to walk out to the street

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:04.170
and I can't take a step out of the house.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.400
For whatever reason,
I'm unable to actually

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.930
leave the house and
shut the door behind me.

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:10.341
'Cause for whatever,

00:08:10.341 --> 00:08:12.270
I was either so egotistical at the moment

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.297
or just believed in myself
so much that I was like,

00:08:14.297 --> 00:08:15.287
"If I go to this interview,

00:08:15.287 --> 00:08:16.787
"I'm gonna get the interview

00:08:16.787 --> 00:08:18.097
"and I'm gonna get the job,

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:18.967
"and they're gonna hire me

00:08:18.967 --> 00:08:20.147
"'cause I'm gonna be so convincing

00:08:20.147 --> 00:08:21.817
"that they're gonna need me.

00:08:21.817 --> 00:08:23.697
"And then I'm gonna stay
here for two, three years

00:08:23.697 --> 00:08:25.937
"and I'm not gonna enjoy what I'm doing.

00:08:25.937 --> 00:08:27.730
"And then I'm gonna
feel trapped and stuck."

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:30.260
And I just didn't want
to go down that route.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.527
So that gave me the courage to say,

00:08:32.527 --> 00:08:34.107
"Okay, I need to go figure
out how to make money

00:08:34.107 --> 00:08:35.790
"on my own because I've
never made a dime."

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:38.510
I wasn't this entrepreneurial
kid doing lemonade stands

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:39.710
and selling baseball cards

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:43.140
and making shoe accessories
or whatever in school,

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.670
I was just trying to play sports.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:49.500
And so I had no entrepreneurial
bone in my body,

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.190
but the necessity to just
survive and be able to contribute

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.610
to rent and pay for my own food

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.630
as a 24 year old at the time,

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:00.430
25 year old became apparent.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:03.210
And that was the push that I needed.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.000
And so I left my sister's house,

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.220
moved to my brother's place

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:08.093
and started paying him, guess how much?

00:09:08.093 --> 00:09:11.241
- 250.
- 250 a month, right!

00:09:11.241 --> 00:09:12.579
(laughing)

00:09:12.579 --> 00:09:14.300
And stayed there for about six months.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.280
And for whatever reason, that investment,

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:20.387
even though it was $250
was a way for me to think,

00:09:20.387 --> 00:09:23.527
"Okay, how do I make 500 a month
so I can get a month ahead?

00:09:23.527 --> 00:09:24.870
"How do I make a thousand a month?"

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:27.150
It just started getting
me thinking differently

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.000
because I was forced to,

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:32.643
and that was 2009 and here we are now.

00:09:34.299 --> 00:09:35.738
- And then it's been an
interesting 11 years.

00:09:35.738 --> 00:09:36.571
- It's been an interesting journey.

00:09:36.571 --> 00:09:38.160
- So you mentioned some
of the growing up stuff

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.360
and self esteem.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.092
So I know it's not the most
fun thing to talk about

00:09:42.092 --> 00:09:43.740
in the world, but you were molested

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.200
when you were five years old.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:46.740
- Yeah.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.230
- Can you just break it down a little bit?

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:51.310
'Cause I think it does
sort of frame a lot--

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.367
- Yeah, it frames a lot.
- The way you look

00:09:53.367 --> 00:09:54.488
at the world, obviously.

00:09:54.488 --> 00:09:55.321
- One in six men have been sexually abused

00:09:55.321 --> 00:09:56.420
in some way in America,

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:59.750
which I had no clue about these
statistics until I started

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:03.180
to open up about my sexual abuse
and started to research it.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:07.740
I just thought I was the only
boy that had ever been abused

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:11.500
and therefore I was
worthless, I was helpless.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.730
And if people actually knew this about me,

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:15.010
they would never love me.

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:16.010
They would never accept me.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:17.730
People would laugh at
me and make fun of me.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:19.390
That was the fear inside.

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.260
And I think we talked about
this a little bit where,

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.710
you know, I can't imagine
what it's like being gay,

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:26.610
coming out, the fear that you might have

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.960
if there's a lot of pressure against you,

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.250
but I think we can, you know,

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:32.083
understand each other in that,

00:10:32.083 --> 00:10:34.920
in that sense of like something
you're afraid to share

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.240
people where you don't
think you'd be accepted.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:38.470
That's where I felt.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:42.270
And I felt like, you know, I was,

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:43.620
when I was five, I was sexually abused,

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.640
and when I was eight, my
brother went to prison

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:46.610
for four and a half years.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:50.580
So I would go visit a prison
visiting room every weekend.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.990
And I remember walking in the first time

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.600
and just being so intimidated and scared.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.928
And we were the only white family there.

00:10:57.928 --> 00:10:59.230
There was a lot of black and Mexican,

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:04.010
Latino families in the visiting
room seeing the inmates.

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:06.400
And I remember thinking to
myself after many months

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.960
I would get to talk to some
of these inmates and just,

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.760
I was a curious eight year old,

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.952
I would just go say hi to people.

00:11:12.952 --> 00:11:14.207
And I was like, "These
inmates are really nice.

00:11:14.207 --> 00:11:15.867
"You know, they're very kind,

00:11:15.867 --> 00:11:19.417
"they're sitting here praying,
they're with their families.

00:11:19.417 --> 00:11:21.460
"They're having good conversations."

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:23.860
Like it made me think
differently about inmates.

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:26.070
Now, some of them had
done really bad things,

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:28.220
were murderers and drug
dealers and things like that.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:31.900
But it got me thinking about
different perspectives of life.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:33.680
However, I wasn't allowed to have friends

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:36.470
during those four years
because the neighborhood kids

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:38.360
weren't allowed to hang out with me.

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:40.840
So I was in a white neighborhood

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.770
and having my brother go to prison,

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:45.980
the parents wouldn't allow
their kids to hang out with me.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.260
- I wasn't sure what you meant there.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:49.350
- So, so I was sexually abused

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.810
and then I just felt
like I was never enough.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:55.570
I was kind of picked on
in school for my dyslexia

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:57.320
and learning abilities.

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:00.360
I was, my brother was in prison
for four and a half years.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.650
So just the neighborhood talk,
don't hang out with Lewis.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:05.620
He's a bad apple also.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.087
So it's just like, it
just, I was just like,

00:12:08.087 --> 00:12:09.530
"Why is this all happening?"

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:12.380
And I think that made me question

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:14.150
a lot of why I was even alive

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:16.120
and I would get in
trouble in school and say,

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:17.460
you know, "I wish I were dead."

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.297
I would say this all the
time to the principal.

00:12:19.297 --> 00:12:21.447
"I wish I were dead,
I wish I wasn't here."

00:12:22.463 --> 00:12:23.650
And so I think I was just
always on the journey

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:26.580
of trying to figure out like,
what's the purpose of my life.

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.300
What's the meaning of everyone's life?

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:31.330
How do we find a place
where we can love ourselves,

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:32.930
accept ourselves for who we are,

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.640
what we've been through
and how can we discover

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:40.360
what our gifts, our purposes, our dreams

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.100
and figure out a way
to make them come true.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:43.480
That's been kind of my journey.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:45.810
- So a lot of that works
at the, at the adult level,

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.010
but for the five-year-old
going through this,

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.600
how long did it last and how did you,

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.260
how did you come out about?

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:55.777
- I think it lasts, it lasts 25 years

00:12:55.777 --> 00:12:59.030
of having the story of the
sexual abuse in my mind

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.230
every single day, like replaying the movie

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:03.063
of it happening every single day.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:07.957
But also kind of deflecting
it a lot too and just saying,

00:13:07.957 --> 00:13:09.547
"Okay, it wasn't that big of a deal.

00:13:09.547 --> 00:13:11.507
"Like it is what it is, it happened.

00:13:11.507 --> 00:13:12.340
"I'm gonna move on.

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:13.687
"Like, don't be such a wuss,

00:13:13.687 --> 00:13:16.560
"like whatever, it's not a big deal."

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.490
And when I was 30, I was at the height

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:23.180
of a lot of things in my career.

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:27.140
I had built and sold a
business for seven figures.

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:28.880
I had started getting accomplishments

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:31.653
and awards as a business entrepreneur.

00:13:32.900 --> 00:13:34.990
You know, I'd played
professional football.

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:37.330
I was on the USA national handball team

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:40.010
trying to make the Olympics,
running a business,

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:43.117
starting this podcast,
like things were happening

00:13:43.117 --> 00:13:46.290
for my career and on an
accomplishment level.

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:47.890
But the more success I achieved,

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:50.000
the more unfulfilled I felt inside.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:51.360
And I didn't understand why.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.197
I just thought, "Okay,
I'm not doing enough.

00:13:53.197 --> 00:13:55.760
"I need to go achieve more."

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:00.760
And I was also, I had also
moved to LA from New York city

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:04.310
for a girl and in a relationship I was in,

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:06.680
and then we didn't work
out really quickly.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.190
The day I moved here,
she broke up with me,

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.980
and just kind of like--
- The day you got here?

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:13.120
- The day I landed.
- Wow.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:14.490
- She kind of like sabotaged it,

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:16.380
she was scared and we got back together

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.060
and tried to make it work,
but it was like an emotional

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:20.120
roller coaster and this
whole kind of next six months

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:23.000
was kind of another re identity,

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:24.070
trying to figure out who am I?

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:25.540
What am I doing here?

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:26.780
What's happening in this relationship?

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:28.860
I just sold this business.

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:31.980
And I just started getting
angry all the time.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.580
I didn't know why I was so angry,

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:36.080
but this was kind of a common theme

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:38.410
throughout my life of being triggered

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:42.160
when I felt taken advantage
of or abused in any situation,

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:43.970
whether it be a personal relationship,

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:46.910
a business partnership or
friendship, a family member.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:49.950
If I felt like someone was abusing me,

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:53.070
it would trigger me and
didn't understand why.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:55.730
And I remember my friend,
Matt, who's here right now.

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:59.610
We started playing
basketball a lot together

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:03.100
on the pickup courts on the
mean streets of Beverly Hills.

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:04.180
- I'm sure I've played on those--

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:05.880
- Super mean streets of
West Hollywood actually.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:06.980
You probably know I'm talking about.

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:08.500
In San Vicente park.
- Oh yeah.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.260
- And we'd play three, four times a week.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:14.360
And for whatever reason, I
would get really aggressive

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:18.470
at the smallest amount
of trash talk, right?

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:20.237
Some 20 year old punk would be like,

00:15:20.237 --> 00:15:22.020
"Oh, this and this about
your mom" or whatever.

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:25.600
And I would be so aggressive
back towards them.

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:27.660
I would scream at them, I
would get in their face.

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.290
I would shove people.

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:33.620
I would try to impose my
intimidation will against everyone

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:36.720
on the court because I felt
under attack constantly.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.790
I didn't know how to have like manage it.

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:42.510
And one day I got in a pretty
bad fight on the basketball

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:44.650
court where I actually
got into a fist fight,

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:46.950
you know, with another guy.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:49.490
And remember at the end of it,

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:51.060
this is right across
from a police station.

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:52.709
- Oh, literally.
- You know what

00:15:52.709 --> 00:15:53.715
I'm talking about?

00:15:53.715 --> 00:15:54.712
Literally across the street.
- On the other side

00:15:54.712 --> 00:15:55.719
of the street, yeah.

00:15:55.719 --> 00:15:57.014
- And I remember thinking to myself--

00:15:57.014 --> 00:15:57.847
- We probably played
together at some point,

00:15:57.847 --> 00:15:58.680
because I used to play there.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:15:59.880
- I might have punched you
in the face for all I know.

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:02.080
No, I'm just kid--
- That is very possible!

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:04.080
I don't trash talk that much.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:05.480
- No yeah, sure.
- I let the place

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:06.659
speak for itself.

00:16:06.659 --> 00:16:07.513
(laughing)

00:16:07.513 --> 00:16:08.998
- You're just dropping threes all day.

00:16:08.998 --> 00:16:12.380
I remember Matt was like, you know,

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:15.970
get out of here because people
are making threats about

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:17.540
like, I'm gonna go to my car and get my,

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:19.850
people were saying crazy stuff
about what they were gonna

00:16:19.850 --> 00:16:22.170
do to me after this.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:24.320
And I remember being like
looking at the police station

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:28.000
being like, I could lose
everything for something stupid,

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.760
getting into a fight as a 30 year old man.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:31.920
Like, what am I doing?

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:32.900
It's a basketball game.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:34.800
We're trying to have fun here.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:37.230
And I remember running back to my home

00:16:39.045 --> 00:16:40.240
and looking in the
mirror and just shaking.

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:41.320
I was just shaking.

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:44.070
I was like wiping off like
the blood on my knuckles.

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:47.150
I was just like looking myself
in the eye for the first time

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:50.170
in a long time, and I
couldn't recognize myself.

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:52.550
And I was just like, who are you?

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:54.030
Who are you, and what are you doing,

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:55.870
and what is wrong with you?

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:59.240
And just looking myself in
the eyes and asking me those,

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:01.950
myself, those questions was
a, was a big eyeopener for me.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:03.350
'Cause I don't think we do this enough.

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:07.870
I don't think we ask ourselves,
who are we, what am I doing?

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:09.310
What is my mission?

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:10.930
Am I doing everything I can do?

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:13.330
- Do you think that's the
most fundamental question?

00:17:13.330 --> 00:17:14.860
I mean, for all the
people you've interviewed

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:16.430
and talked to you and
coached and all that,

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:19.980
that that right there, is
it more than anything else?

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:23.030
- I think the great
individuals in the world

00:17:23.030 --> 00:17:26.200
are clear on their vision and they,

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:29.290
they know exactly what they
want and why they want it.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.050
I didn't know what I
wanted and why I wanted it.

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:33.560
I knew I wanted to prove people wrong.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:34.760
I wanted to look good.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:35.780
I wanted to be right.

00:17:35.780 --> 00:17:37.450
I wanted to make money.

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:39.340
I wanted to be successful,

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:41.730
but it wasn't for a greater purpose.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:45.780
It was for me to look good
and to prove the person

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:47.090
who sexually abused me wrong,

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:49.260
to prove the kids that laughed
at me when I was reading

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:51.970
aloud in class wrong, to
prove the kids that picked

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:54.440
me last in a sport wrong.

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:56.960
It was always to prove people wrong,

00:17:56.960 --> 00:17:58.370
not to lift others up.

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:01.840
And it's really challenging
to constantly want to tear

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:03.920
someone else down and other people down.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:06.460
It's exhausting, constantly doing this.

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:08.060
You're seeing this in the
political space right now

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:09.360
where people are just
attacking each other.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:12.280
I'm like, that's gotta be
exhausting at the end of the night

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:14.860
to just like go to sleep
when you're that much rage

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:16.880
and anger towards something else.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.860
And I'm all for fighting
for what you believe in,

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:22.040
and fighting for a great cause

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:25.370
and fighting for equality
and all these things,

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:28.960
but to aggressively want
to prove others wrong,

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:32.580
in my opinion is a painful act.

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:36.280
And I'd rather just focus on a
vision and try to lift others

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:38.100
up with you in that vision.

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:39.670
So I think that's what a lot of people

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:41.530
have that I've interviewed is,

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:43.940
the great ones, find a clear vision.

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:45.360
They accept themselves fully.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:47.630
They know they're not perfect
'cause none of us are.

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:51.020
They have come to
acceptance with their past,

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:52.030
the wrongs they've done,

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:54.237
the things that they're not proud of,

00:18:54.237 --> 00:18:56.240
but they've accepted it,
and they've moved towards

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:59.510
something greater than
just seeking success.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:02.190
'Cause success is all about us.

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:03.930
Greatness is about others.

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:06.490
Success is about what I can
do for myself to look good.

00:19:06.490 --> 00:19:09.097
Greatness is what I can do for
other people to lift them up.

00:19:09.097 --> 00:19:11.200
And I think that's when
you transition from just

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.640
accomplishing something
as a great athlete,

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:15.940
towards transitioning
into something greater

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:17.900
or accomplishing as a
great business leader

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:21.030
towards how can I truly
serve my employees,

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:23.070
serve my clients and customers,

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:24.497
and then serve the world around me.

00:19:24.497 --> 00:19:26.340
And I think that's the difference

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.160
between success and greatness.

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:31.430
- That's a pretty solid
Segway for about 30 reasons

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:34.760
to get to a little bit of
your chat with Kobe Bryant,

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:36.310
because I think it encapsulates

00:19:37.656 --> 00:19:38.630
so much of what you're talking about.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:40.680
So I watched it again this morning

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:42.850
and we talked about it
when I was on your show.

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:45.740
And I hadn't seen the
interview of you and Kobe.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:47.830
And what I said to you in that interview

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:52.070
was that this was just a few
weeks after, after he died,

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:54.730
is that it felt like it was
like a rip in the universe

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:58.470
because it's hard to fathom,
putting aside the tragedy

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:00.800
of all the other people and
his daughter, of course,

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:02.740
but those aren't public
people that we know,

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:05.781
but of course it's as, as
equal a tragedy obviously.

00:20:05.781 --> 00:20:08.530
But there was something
about Kobe that it was like,

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:10.540
I told you, I was watching sports,

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:13.980
you know, the spectrum Lakers
channel the day before.

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:16.320
And I saw him and he was
just being interviewed

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:17.670
and he's in a suit and he's got the beard

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:19.138
and he looks really sharp.

00:20:19.138 --> 00:20:20.770
And I was like, man, that guy's
just living his best life.

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:22.702
That was literally the day before.

00:20:22.702 --> 00:20:23.535
- Wow.

00:20:23.535 --> 00:20:26.090
- And it feels like a rip in the universe

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:28.040
when someone who's doing all of the things

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:29.840
that you just described,

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:31.820
who has reached the
pinnacle of their sport,

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:34.610
but then flourished after
and started this basketball

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:36.840
Academy and a gajillion other things,

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:39.480
then disappears from the earth.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.880
So can you just talk a little
bit about what it was like

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:46.170
to sit down with Kobe
and what it taught you

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:48.460
and did he have all of those pieces?

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:50.010
'Cause, literally just everything

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:51.120
that you just laid out right there,

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:52.720
that's what he was talking
about in the interview.

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:54.480
And you could see it was real,

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:56.418
there was no BS behind it--

00:20:56.418 --> 00:20:57.576
- Yeah.
- You really could.

00:20:57.576 --> 00:20:58.409
- It was a powerful experience.

00:20:58.409 --> 00:21:00.340
The whole, the whole
setup of it was powerful.

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:02.440
We booked the interview the day before.

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:04.200
So I didn't really have much time to prep,

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:05.400
which was probably a good thing for me.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:07.020
'Cause I didn't have like
two weeks to think about it.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:08.579
I've got Kobe.

00:21:08.579 --> 00:21:10.559
It was like literally
booked it at six p.m.

00:21:10.559 --> 00:21:11.743
I think the night before.

00:21:11.743 --> 00:21:12.790
And then I was there at
six a.m. the next morning

00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:14.680
in his office in the OC.

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:17.780
And I remember the most
powerful thing he taught

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:20.900
me was not what he said, but his example.

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:23.510
And what I mean by that is
when we got to his office,

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:27.400
I think it was probably around 6:15, 6:30,

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:30.280
the interview was at eight
o'clock and I knew I was,

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:33.310
the publicist was like,
"You got 20 minutes."

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:34.830
I think she originally said,
"You've got 15 minutes,"

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:36.050
but I was like, "Please give me 20."

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:37.900
Right, when I asked two questions.

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:39.793
Yeah, we get there.

00:21:40.901 --> 00:21:43.280
And the assistant opens the door for us,

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:45.550
and there's a big office
space in the Orange County.

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:47.670
And she says, "Here's
where we normally film."

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:48.547
But in my mind I was like,

00:21:48.547 --> 00:21:50.381
"Yeah, it doesn't really look that good.

00:21:50.381 --> 00:21:51.478
"Can I look around the office

00:21:51.478 --> 00:21:52.311
"and see if there's another spot

00:21:52.311 --> 00:21:53.800
"we can set up our cameras?"

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:55.510
So we walked through the office

00:21:55.510 --> 00:21:59.810
and we go down kind of this
long hall with conference rooms

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:02.680
on each side of the
hallway with glass windows,

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.270
you can see inside the conference rooms.

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:09.180
And then it walks down into
another big open space of more

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:11.890
office chairs and things like that.

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:14.670
We walked down, I see the other room.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:16.007
I'm like, "I don't really
like anything here.

00:22:16.007 --> 00:22:18.460
"Let's go back to the original space."

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:21.820
So we walked back
through the glass hallway

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:25.510
and the last window on the left, I see it.

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:27.880
The lights are off in
the back of the room,

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:29.190
probably 20, 30 feet back.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:33.350
I see a shadow of a man
like this looking up.

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:35.160
Just looking up at the ceiling.

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:36.580
And no computer screen was on,

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:37.980
there was no phone in his hand.

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:40.560
And I look real quick
double-take and I see it's Kobe

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.550
sitting there like this, just looking up.

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:45.570
This is 6:15 in the morning.

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:47.480
And I'm thinking," Our
interview's not till eight."

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:50.020
And I'm thinking to myself,
I asked the assistant,

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:51.210
I go, "Is that Kobe?"

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:52.169
She goes, "Yeah."

00:22:52.169 --> 00:22:53.997
And I go, "What's he doing here so early?

00:22:53.997 --> 00:22:55.197
"Like this is his company.

00:22:55.197 --> 00:22:57.310
"This is his space, why is he here?"

00:22:57.310 --> 00:22:58.900
She goes, "He's been
here for almost an hour."

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:00.770
And he was at the gym with his daughter.

00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:04.200
The one who passed away, at
four a.m. working out with her.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.157
And he comes here, he's
always the first one here.

00:23:06.157 --> 00:23:07.940
And he's always the most prepared.

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:12.170
And I'll go, just that
moment alone of seeing Kobe

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:13.940
as a shadow, looking up.

00:23:13.940 --> 00:23:16.410
And I can only imagine he
was visualizing his dream.

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.790
He was going over, you know,

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:20.390
his game film for the day,

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:22.890
he was planning what he
was gonna be talking about.

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:24.330
There was an interview with me afterwards,

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:25.640
with Lil Wayne who was there.

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:27.060
So they were sitting up in another part.

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:28.500
It was kind of surreal just morning.

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:29.630
- Yeah.
- Lil Wayne and all

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:32.580
his body guards and this
whole crew was there.

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:35.040
I'm there with one person
setting up our three cameras

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:37.280
and we're like, "Hey," you know?

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:39.680
And I was just like, "This is surreal."

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:43.810
And about an hour, about 15
minutes before our interview,

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:45.710
so for another hour, hour and half,

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:47.530
the lights are off in this room.

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:49.770
And now people starting
to come in around 8:30

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:51.500
in the office, and so there's some,

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:53.310
there's some activity happening.

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:55.410
And I'm sitting there in the
chair, just kind of waiting,

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:56.950
just trying to like be ready.

00:23:56.950 --> 00:23:58.300
I don't know if he's gonna come out early.

00:23:58.300 --> 00:23:59.440
Is it coming out on time?

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:00.880
I don't know what's happening.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:04.008
He ends up coming out
probably two minutes before

00:24:04.008 --> 00:24:06.033
eight o'clock start time.

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:12.360
And the publicist gave me a
bunch of papers of everything

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:14.630
not to ask him.

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:16.560
A list of things you cannot talk about

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.810
from his past, or parents
to all these things.

00:24:18.810 --> 00:24:20.350
Like don't mention this,
don't mention this.

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:22.450
I mean a list, I've never had this before.

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:28.020
And so I know to myself, I've
got two minutes to connect

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:30.550
with arguably the greatest of all time,

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:33.350
who's just won an Oscar
as well, by the way.

00:24:33.350 --> 00:24:36.590
And two minutes to connect before

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:38.560
our 20 minute interview, right?

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:39.870
What am I gonna say?

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:42.290
And hopefully he doesn't,
you know when you meet people

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:44.885
that are really famous and
you're inspired by them,

00:24:44.885 --> 00:24:45.718
and you meet them and you're like,

00:24:45.718 --> 00:24:47.572
"Gosh, I'm really let down by them."

00:24:47.572 --> 00:24:48.405
- They say, never meet your heroes.

00:24:48.405 --> 00:24:49.238
- Right?

00:24:49.238 --> 00:24:50.660
And this guy was the complete opposite.

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:54.190
He was right away came up to
me, gave me a big handshake,

00:24:54.190 --> 00:24:56.532
and was just like, "Hey, thank
you so much for being here,

00:24:56.532 --> 00:24:57.365
"it really means a lot to me.

00:24:57.365 --> 00:24:59.097
"We're trying to get the
message out about our podcast.

00:24:59.097 --> 00:25:00.950
"Like thank you for taking the time."

00:25:00.950 --> 00:25:02.180
Looked me in the eyes.

00:25:02.180 --> 00:25:04.517
First thing I said to him was like,

00:25:04.517 --> 00:25:05.517
"I just want to say, thank you.

00:25:05.517 --> 00:25:08.717
"I work with, I'm friends
with a lot of Olympians.

00:25:08.717 --> 00:25:10.567
"And all of them say that during the last

00:25:10.567 --> 00:25:13.387
"couple of Olympics, you
were the most giving athlete

00:25:13.387 --> 00:25:16.137
"that you would show up
to other sporting events,

00:25:16.137 --> 00:25:18.887
"that you would take photos
with everyone in the, you know,

00:25:18.887 --> 00:25:21.117
"in the village and during lunches

00:25:21.117 --> 00:25:22.697
"that you were just so kind and giving.

00:25:22.697 --> 00:25:25.027
"So as a USA handball player,

00:25:25.027 --> 00:25:27.177
"I want to thank you for how you show

00:25:27.177 --> 00:25:28.690
"up during those times."

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:31.260
And right away, he goes,
"You play handball?"

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:32.870
And I go, "Yeah, I love handball."

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:34.527
And he goes, "I love hand ball,

00:25:34.527 --> 00:25:36.347
"playing it in Italy growing up,

00:25:36.347 --> 00:25:39.170
"that's all I did as a fun activity."

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:40.897
And I go, "Amazing, yeah,
it's such a great sport.

00:25:40.897 --> 00:25:43.600
"I wish we had it here, the USA more."

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:46.990
And then I mentioned a couple of people

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:48.627
we had in common and he's like,

00:25:48.627 --> 00:25:50.537
"Man, those guys are my brothers.

00:25:50.537 --> 00:25:51.985
"Like, I love them.

00:25:51.985 --> 00:25:53.400
"I'm glad we have some good connections."

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:54.440
And I said one more thing.

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:58.627
I said, "You know, your team
has told me what not to share

00:25:58.627 --> 00:25:59.557
"and what not talk about.

00:25:59.557 --> 00:26:01.267
"Is there anything else off limits

00:26:01.267 --> 00:26:02.560
"that I shouldn't talk about?"

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:05.797
And he said, "Rip that up,
ask me anything you want

00:26:05.797 --> 00:26:07.640
"and take as long as you want."

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:09.380
So I was only supposed to have 20 minutes.

00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:11.560
I think we went 40 or 45,

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:12.720
and I tried to be respectful,

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:13.997
'cause the publicist kept saying like,

00:26:13.997 --> 00:26:15.350
"Wrap it up," in the background.

00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:17.400
And I was like, "Okay,
let me be respectful."

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:20.240
But even the last few
questions he answered

00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:22.040
where he's talking about
like his whole mission

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:26.020
was to tell great stories
and him talking about love

00:26:26.020 --> 00:26:28.580
and family, for me, it
was really inspiring.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:32.020
Not only the words he
says, but his example.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:35.390
He isn't just saying work
hard and outwork everyone,

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:37.760
he's actually showed me as an example.

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:41.790
I didn't know if he was
gonna see me seeing him

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:44.410
that morning, like he didn't
think I was gonna walk

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:46.260
past his office 'cause
I wasn't supposed to.

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:47.867
- Right.
- But he was there.

00:26:47.867 --> 00:26:50.310
And just that example of, I mean,

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:52.103
I don't get there to my place

00:26:52.103 --> 00:26:53.220
30 minutes before my interviews, right?

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:55.820
You, I guess, I mean you
come in when you're ready,

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:59.120
but it's like just that
example of hard work

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:00.910
it shows why he was so great.

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:02.680
And that was inspiring to me.

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:04.822
- Isn't it interesting that, you know,

00:27:04.822 --> 00:27:05.655
they say the same thing
about Michael Jordan.

00:27:05.655 --> 00:27:06.750
He outworked everybody.

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:08.020
He practiced the hardest,

00:27:08.020 --> 00:27:09.907
like you'd think that every
athlete would be like,

00:27:09.907 --> 00:27:11.750
"Oh, that's the obvious way to do it."

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:13.760
Or forget athletes, that
all of us in our lives

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:15.957
would be like, "Oh, you want
to be the best at what you do?

00:27:15.957 --> 00:27:17.097
"You got to work the hardest.

00:27:17.097 --> 00:27:18.137
"You've got to show up first

00:27:18.137 --> 00:27:20.550
"and you got to leave the
last and blah, blah, blah."

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:24.410
And yet 99.999% of us don't do it.

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:27.827
Even though you look at the
guys that you admire most

00:27:27.827 --> 00:27:30.060
and they're the ones doing it.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.480
- It's a big sacrifice.

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:34.000
It's a lot of time, a
lot of energy in you.

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:37.390
You have to give up so much
to go all in on one thing.

00:27:37.390 --> 00:27:38.810
So I know why it's hard to do.

00:27:38.810 --> 00:27:41.060
I mean, it's like he was up at four a.m.

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:42.030
working out with his daughter

00:27:42.030 --> 00:27:44.360
and then he was at the office
at 5:30 or whatever it was,

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:46.368
or six or whatever it was.

00:27:46.368 --> 00:27:47.510
And it's like, it's a sacrifice.

00:27:47.510 --> 00:27:50.360
We'd much rather be
comfortable and sleep more

00:27:50.360 --> 00:27:52.560
and watch Netflix more.

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:54.400
I like watching Netflix.

00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:57.360
It's not easy to be great.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:59.570
It's very challenging.

00:27:59.570 --> 00:28:02.060
The commitment, the consistency over time.

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:03.560
And that's one of the things Kobe said,

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:05.217
he's just like, "It's simple math.

00:28:05.217 --> 00:28:07.197
"I just put in double the
hours as everyone else,

00:28:07.197 --> 00:28:09.090
"when I was a teenager."

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:10.610
The first year he started
playing basketball

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:13.590
on a summer camp, he didn't
score one point in this whole,

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:16.950
like summer and this
tournament that he played in.

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:19.510
And then he started doing double
the hours and the next year

00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:21.080
he was like the highest scorer.

00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:22.397
And he was like, "It was simple math.

00:28:22.397 --> 00:28:23.927
"I wasn't more talented.

00:28:23.927 --> 00:28:26.130
"I simply put in more hours."

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:27.770
- It's a good transition to something else

00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:30.000
that he said that I think is
interesting where he talked,

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.430
you were asking him about when he was sort

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:33.810
of hitting his stride.

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:36.680
And he talks about this
moment where Shaq is injured

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:38.830
for like eight games, and
he scored, I think 40,

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:41.010
over 40 in eight straight
games or something like that.

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:43.660
And then Phil Jackson
calls him into the office

00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:45.882
when Shaq comes back
and basically is like,

00:28:45.882 --> 00:28:50.007
"Now you got to tone it down
because now Shaq is back

00:28:50.007 --> 00:28:51.347
"and the East plays different.

00:28:51.347 --> 00:28:52.807
"So when we get to the finals,

00:28:52.807 --> 00:28:54.360
"we're gonna do this differently."

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:56.957
And he, you know, you'd think
that he would just be like,

00:28:56.957 --> 00:28:59.807
"No, we're winning like crazy."

00:28:59.807 --> 00:29:01.880
"I'm better than Shaq,
we don't even need Shaq."

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:03.160
You know, they had their
own problems later,

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:04.897
but, he basically was like,

00:29:04.897 --> 00:29:07.220
"Yeah, so I listened to the mentor."

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:08.190
That's what I wanted to get to,

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:10.070
'cause you talk a lot about mentorship.

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:12.380
He listened to the mentor
in this case, Phil Jackson,

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:15.280
who had already done this with
Jordan and he dialed it back.

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:17.430
And then of course they ended
up winning a championship.

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:18.886
- Exactly, yeah.

00:29:18.886 --> 00:29:20.640
I think the coaches and mentors in my life

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:23.570
have been instrumental towards my success

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:26.230
through every stage, from
my parents being mentors

00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:27.960
and coaches to my older siblings,

00:29:27.960 --> 00:29:31.420
to teachers and coaches in school.

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:33.690
- Do you sense we have a
major lack of that right now?

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:35.640
I mean, one of the things
I keep saying on Twitter

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:37.550
and every time I'm on TV now is,

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:39.820
it seems like we don't have
a lot of adults in the room.

00:29:39.820 --> 00:29:41.430
Not, not at an individual level,

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:43.107
of course there are mentors.

00:29:43.107 --> 00:29:44.820
And we talked about, I wrote
a whole chapter about Jordan

00:29:44.820 --> 00:29:46.363
Peterson in my book.

00:29:46.363 --> 00:29:47.930
So it's not, it's not that
these people don't exist,

00:29:47.930 --> 00:29:49.430
but it seems like in the public sphere,

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:51.540
we don't have a lot of people
right now that you can look

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:54.167
to and be like, "Oh,
they're modeling something.

00:29:54.167 --> 00:29:56.040
"That is the way I want to be."

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:56.873
Something like that.

00:29:56.873 --> 00:29:58.990
- I think it's hard to
have the courage to reach

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:01.500
out to mentors in the first
place and to identify like,

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:03.910
okay, The Rock.

00:30:03.910 --> 00:30:05.780
I would love The Rock
to be my mentor, right?

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:08.540
Or Jordan Peterson for a young adult,

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:11.146
who's like, I'd love for
Jordan Peterson to be my mentor

00:30:11.146 --> 00:30:12.030
and get in touch with him.

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:13.960
It may seem intimidating
to reach out to someone,

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:15.670
how do I get in touch with them?

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:17.360
Luckily, Jordan Peterson has a great book

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:21.020
that you could be mentored by
his talks online, his podcast.

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:22.240
You can go to his tours,

00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:25.010
you can learn enough from
someone's book to hopefully

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:26.700
implement it and learn from their example

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:27.533
of what they're doing.

00:30:27.533 --> 00:30:31.670
I really look at my model
as Oprah meets The Rock,

00:30:31.670 --> 00:30:33.410
meets LeBron James.

00:30:33.410 --> 00:30:35.137
And I'm trying to say,
"Okay, I want to learn

00:30:35.137 --> 00:30:37.267
"from each one of those
and create my own model

00:30:37.267 --> 00:30:39.990
"of success for my
mission and my purpose."

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:41.660
I feel like I'm a blend of all three.

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:44.310
I've got the athlete in LeBron James,

00:30:44.310 --> 00:30:47.275
obviously not as half as good
an athlete as him, but that,

00:30:47.275 --> 00:30:50.880
that Ohio brand that wanted
to give back to schools.

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:53.260
We, we donate a lot of money
to build schools for kids

00:30:53.260 --> 00:30:54.630
in different countries.

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:57.790
So I see that model as like
"I'm a USA handball player."

00:30:57.790 --> 00:30:59.870
I see The Rock as a former
professional football player

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:01.650
who's now into entertainment.

00:31:01.650 --> 00:31:04.350
I played former football,

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:06.720
I was former pro, now into
kind of entertainment.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:09.220
And then Oprah, who just is
a curator and facilitator

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:11.162
of ideas and people.

00:31:11.162 --> 00:31:11.995
And that's what I'm doing as well.

00:31:11.995 --> 00:31:14.030
So I try to find my mentors,

00:31:14.030 --> 00:31:15.930
even though I've never met any of them,

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:18.100
I try to find them and model them.

00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:20.760
But I have lots of coaches that I pay for,

00:31:20.760 --> 00:31:24.550
in business, relationships,
my fitness and health

00:31:24.550 --> 00:31:26.800
and just life.

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:31.150
For me, I'm such a huge
advocate of coaching.

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:33.820
Coaching is what Michael Jordan,

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:36.270
Kobe Bryant and LeBron
James all talk about,

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:40.420
is the key to what made them
great athletes in school.

00:31:40.420 --> 00:31:41.980
To now as professional athletes,

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:46.010
you cannot win in sports,
in business and in life,

00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:47.800
without having great
mentorship and coaches.

00:31:47.800 --> 00:31:49.250
I just don't think you can.

00:31:49.250 --> 00:31:53.410
You can get a far, but I
don't think you can sustain

00:31:53.410 --> 00:31:56.000
that success over time without consistent

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:57.930
mentorship and guidance.

00:31:57.930 --> 00:31:59.440
That's just my belief.

00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:02.360
- So I had Burgess Owen
a couple of weeks ago,

00:32:02.360 --> 00:32:06.120
who's a former NFL player
and he's now running

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:08.160
for Congress in Utah.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:09.900
And he actually talked about
a lot of the similar things.

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:11.267
One of the things I asked him was,

00:32:11.267 --> 00:32:13.017
"Do you think there's a connection

00:32:13.017 --> 00:32:16.227
"between being a professional athlete

00:32:16.227 --> 00:32:18.797
"and someone that can
compete at an elite level

00:32:18.797 --> 00:32:20.687
"and then someone that can be a success

00:32:20.687 --> 00:32:23.567
"on the other side, because
you're just using those,

00:32:23.567 --> 00:32:25.320
"those disciplines?"
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:26.640
- You obviously agree with that.

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:27.920
- Yeah.

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:29.040
I think there's a lot of great athletes

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:32.250
who can transition and
then others who stay stuck

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:33.810
in their past identity.

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:35.687
They say, "I'm gonna
live off the past dream

00:32:35.687 --> 00:32:37.287
"that I had, me as a great athlete.

00:32:37.287 --> 00:32:39.480
"I'm gonna live off the money."

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:42.227
Where Kobe said, "Okay,
I've got all this money.

00:32:42.227 --> 00:32:43.610
"I could retire now."

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:45.427
But he said, "I want to do
something great with my life

00:32:45.427 --> 00:32:46.577
"in the next chapter.

00:32:46.577 --> 00:32:49.017
"20 seasons playing the NBA.

00:32:49.017 --> 00:32:50.543
"What do I want to do now?

00:32:50.543 --> 00:32:51.376
"I want to tell great stories

00:32:51.376 --> 00:32:52.827
"'cause that's what's
gonna change the world.

00:32:52.827 --> 00:32:54.337
"Me not being a great athlete anymore

00:32:54.337 --> 00:32:55.217
"is gonna change the world.

00:32:55.217 --> 00:32:59.657
"Me creating content to tell
stories that reaches kids,

00:32:59.657 --> 00:33:03.197
"young adults, adults, that's
what will change people's

00:33:03.197 --> 00:33:05.970
"belief in themselves and the world."

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:07.647
So I think his ability to say,

00:33:07.647 --> 00:33:10.097
"I'm gonna use these
disciplines in my business

00:33:10.097 --> 00:33:11.270
"and figure it out."

00:33:11.270 --> 00:33:13.660
He also had coaches and
partners in his business

00:33:13.660 --> 00:33:15.810
that helped him and guided him.

00:33:15.810 --> 00:33:16.900
And I think that's the key.

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:18.250
A lot of guys don't transition

00:33:18.250 --> 00:33:19.990
with great coaching afterwards,

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:23.030
and you got to seek out
coaches or mentors constantly

00:33:23.030 --> 00:33:24.310
because we're always facing new chapters.

00:33:24.310 --> 00:33:25.830
You've got your business model right now,

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:29.460
but I know you've got big
goals and big plans ahead.

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:31.640
When you launched your book
and you didn't have a book

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:33.857
before you probably seeked
out other authors and said,

00:33:33.857 --> 00:33:35.057
"Hey, what should I do here?

00:33:35.057 --> 00:33:37.257
"What should, Jordan tell me how you sold

00:33:37.257 --> 00:33:39.542
"a million copies in six months," right?

00:33:39.542 --> 00:33:40.864
"What was the plan?

00:33:40.864 --> 00:33:41.862
"What was the strategy?"

00:33:41.862 --> 00:33:43.790
You're seeking mentors, whether
you're officially paying

00:33:43.790 --> 00:33:46.780
for coaches or not, and you're
seeking it at every stage

00:33:46.780 --> 00:33:48.360
and that's what makes you a great success.

00:33:48.360 --> 00:33:50.730
- I described in the chapter about Jordan,

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:52.480
he was an accidental mentor.

00:33:52.480 --> 00:33:53.930
We just started doing stuff together.

00:33:53.930 --> 00:33:56.067
And then I was like, "Holy cow, this guy,

00:33:56.067 --> 00:33:57.957
"he's changing the world
right in front of me.

00:33:57.957 --> 00:34:00.700
"And I have the honor of being
that close to this thing?"

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:03.140
- Access, like every night, right?

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:04.602
For how long?

00:34:04.602 --> 00:34:05.600
A year, two years?

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:06.605
- Like a year and a half.

00:34:06.605 --> 00:34:09.817
And the crazy part for me
was that he never once went

00:34:11.140 --> 00:34:12.530
backwards on one of those rules.

00:34:12.530 --> 00:34:15.110
I never saw the guy not
live up to the things

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:15.943
he was talking about.

00:34:15.943 --> 00:34:18.980
How much of this stuff do you
think is sort of hardwired

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:21.840
in the way people are born versus learn?

00:34:21.840 --> 00:34:23.720
Because like on the Kobe level,

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:26.390
I always think about Tim Duncan
who retired the same year

00:34:26.390 --> 00:34:30.380
Kobe did, you know, he won
five championships himself,

00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:31.600
but he was quiet.

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:33.150
You didn't know anything about him.

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:34.380
There was no, I can't even picture

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:36.363
him showboating or yelling.

00:34:37.226 --> 00:34:38.190
You can barely hear his voice.

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:40.310
You know, I've seen him be
interviewed a million times,

00:34:40.310 --> 00:34:41.820
but like Kobe, you can like, you know,

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:44.640
hear his voice that he had.

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:46.920
He clearly worked hard.

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:48.750
He was a total pro the whole thing.

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:50.940
Even the way he retired,
like Kobe's retirement,

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:52.390
it was very public.

00:34:52.390 --> 00:34:54.050
- It was a show, it was a performance.

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:55.670
- It was like Kareem, it was a show.

00:34:55.670 --> 00:34:57.430
- I'll drop the mic, everything, yeah.

00:34:57.430 --> 00:34:59.550
- The whole thing, like
everywhere he went, it was like,

00:34:59.550 --> 00:35:01.770
this is Kobe's last night in Philadelphia.

00:35:01.770 --> 00:35:04.650
Where Tim Duncan again, the same thing,

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:07.560
like all the success, all
that stuff, the great numbers,

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:10.300
but just kinda just was like
quiet about the whole thing.

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:13.950
How much of just like the,
all of it do you think

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:16.070
is just hard wiring versus--

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:17.443
- Environment?
- Or environment,

00:35:17.443 --> 00:35:18.276
everything else?

00:35:18.276 --> 00:35:21.020
- I think it's a little bit
of both because you know,

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:23.310
I don't think I would
have been a great athlete

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:26.020
had I not felt abused all the time.

00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:28.870
So I think my environment of sexual abuse,

00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:32.130
feeling left behind, feeling
made fun of in school.

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:33.640
Being picked last in sports.

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:35.160
In fourth grade, I was
picked last in the sports

00:35:35.160 --> 00:35:37.080
team over girls.

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:38.500
And that was probably
the most like humiliating

00:35:38.500 --> 00:35:41.210
time as a fourth grader of being like,

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:46.210
okay, we're in a recess
here and on the playground.

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:48.740
And there was a dodgeball game.

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:50.663
And that our class was gonna do,

00:35:50.663 --> 00:35:53.223
it's probably 30 kids in
our class, maybe 40 kids.

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:56.400
Call it half man, half women, right?

00:35:56.400 --> 00:36:00.660
And I remember there was
two captains of the game,

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:03.220
and both the captains were
picking one at a time, right?

00:36:03.220 --> 00:36:04.770
You pick your top athlete.

00:36:04.770 --> 00:36:06.160
And they picked all the guys.

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:09.520
And then I was the last guy, boy standing.

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:12.030
And then they start
picking girls one by one.

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:14.190
And then they pick the last girl.

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:16.860
This is like a girl who is, you know,

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:20.060
in glasses and like no
athletic bone in her body.

00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:23.020
And they pick her and
they don't even pick me.

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:25.010
This is a memory, right.

00:36:25.010 --> 00:36:26.470
They don't want to pick me.

00:36:26.470 --> 00:36:28.360
I'm just by default, the
last person that goes

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:30.060
on the last team.

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:31.247
And I'm thinking to myself,

00:36:31.247 --> 00:36:33.127
"I know I'm not the worst athlete here.

00:36:33.127 --> 00:36:34.510
"This is fourth grade."

00:36:34.510 --> 00:36:37.760
And I remember being
like these experiences

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:39.730
that we have shape us.

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:42.640
And they shaped me into
saying never again,

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:44.400
am I gonna feel this humiliated.

00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:45.910
In that moment, I remember
saying never again

00:36:45.910 --> 00:36:47.310
am I'm gonna be picked last.

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:50.650
I am going to the gym
every day after school.

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:53.190
And you're gonna stay
there till my mom calls

00:36:53.190 --> 00:36:56.400
me home to eat dinner and just work out

00:36:56.400 --> 00:36:59.080
and play basketball and play this sport.

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:02.100
That drove me, that experience.

00:37:02.100 --> 00:37:03.760
You know, I don't know if
you have similar experiences

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:06.070
in your life where
maybe you felt neglected

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:07.610
or felt like taken advantage of,

00:37:07.610 --> 00:37:09.700
or felt, you know,

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:11.693
in just because of whatever,

00:37:12.742 --> 00:37:15.302
that made you say, "I'm gonna show you,

00:37:15.302 --> 00:37:16.200
"I'm gonna prove you wrong."

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:18.610
And that drive, I think
Kobe had a lot of that drive

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:21.160
where he wanted to prove
people wrong as well.

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:22.630
And then it transitions to, okay,

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:24.510
this doesn't work for me anymore.

00:37:24.510 --> 00:37:27.310
This energy works to a
certain point of achieving,

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:28.820
but I still feel empty.

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:29.760
Why?

00:37:29.760 --> 00:37:31.670
And that's where I had that
kind of moment when I was 30.

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:33.120
I was like, I'm achieving a lot.

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:34.670
I've got money, I've got success,

00:37:34.670 --> 00:37:37.100
but why am I feeling alone?

00:37:37.100 --> 00:37:38.440
Why do I feel so angry still?

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:40.423
Like, shouldn't I feel happier?

00:37:41.360 --> 00:37:43.440
And that's what allowed me to reflect.

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:46.320
And I went to a, I started
doing a lot of work on myself.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:48.710
I started reaching out to
therapists, coaches, mentors,

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:51.400
spiritual guides, going to workshops.

00:37:51.400 --> 00:37:53.393
And during that time of a workshop,

00:37:54.625 --> 00:37:56.680
a lot of people were
opening up about their past.

00:37:56.680 --> 00:37:59.987
And I remember the workshop
trainer facilitator was like,

00:37:59.987 --> 00:38:01.750
"We are gonna be moving forward."

00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:04.644
It was after about a few days
in this workshop, he said,

00:38:04.644 --> 00:38:06.641
"We're gonna be moving
forward to get clear

00:38:06.641 --> 00:38:07.887
"about what we want to
create for our future.

00:38:07.887 --> 00:38:10.737
"But in order to get clear about
our mission and our future,

00:38:10.737 --> 00:38:13.627
"and to be able to tackle
it with our full energy,

00:38:13.627 --> 00:38:16.797
"we must clear the past
and forgive ourselves

00:38:16.797 --> 00:38:18.647
"or forgive anything that's
happened in the past.

00:38:18.647 --> 00:38:20.837
"Otherwise we're gonna be
holding onto this weight

00:38:20.837 --> 00:38:22.785
"that's dragging us down."

00:38:22.785 --> 00:38:27.320
I remember sitting there
thinking to myself, "Huh."

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:29.270
I feel like I've addressed
everything in my past.

00:38:29.270 --> 00:38:30.390
You know, I've talked about my brother

00:38:30.390 --> 00:38:32.130
being in prison and what that felt like,

00:38:32.130 --> 00:38:34.840
I've talked about being picked
on and bullied in school.

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:39.040
I talked about, you know,
just my parents arguing

00:38:39.040 --> 00:38:40.070
and them getting a divorce,

00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:41.530
just like everyone else in the room.

00:38:41.530 --> 00:38:43.680
You know, nothing's new here.

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:45.570
I feel like everyone's gone
through similar experiences.

00:38:45.570 --> 00:38:47.740
So I don't know what else to talk about.

00:38:47.740 --> 00:38:51.270
And then the memory of
being sexually abused

00:38:51.270 --> 00:38:53.897
went in my mind, and I thought to myself,

00:38:53.897 --> 00:38:57.267
"Why have I never shared
this with anyone in 25 years?

00:38:57.267 --> 00:39:01.087
"Why have I kept this in and
been afraid to talk about it?"

00:39:02.270 --> 00:39:03.370
And for whatever reason,

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:05.650
the environment of the
moment, the workshop

00:39:05.650 --> 00:39:07.957
and people opening up, I was like,

00:39:07.957 --> 00:39:09.217
"If I don't share this now,

00:39:09.217 --> 00:39:11.690
"I'll probably never share
this, it'll go to my grave."

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:14.140
So I just stood up in front of the room.

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:17.410
And I remember I couldn't look
up at anyone in their eyes.

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:19.620
I was so ashamed of
what I was about to say,

00:39:19.620 --> 00:39:21.650
so I just looked down at the carpet

00:39:21.650 --> 00:39:23.700
and I just walked
through the entire event.

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:24.930
You know, when I was five years old,

00:39:24.930 --> 00:39:27.028
I was at the babysitter's,

00:39:27.028 --> 00:39:30.330
the babysitter had a son, a late teenager,

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:32.870
and he took me into the
bathroom and he raped me.

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:35.310
And I just shared the
story for the first time.

00:39:35.310 --> 00:39:39.840
And I remember sitting down,
the whole room's silent.

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:41.370
I think people were just in shock

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:45.390
to see a straight white male,

00:39:45.390 --> 00:39:49.760
who's this jock-looking
athlete, big guy, strong guy.

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:52.930
You know, probably had a
little bit of ego showing,

00:39:52.930 --> 00:39:56.280
showing up during that
time, share that story.

00:39:56.280 --> 00:39:57.480
'Cause I never heard, personally,

00:39:57.480 --> 00:39:59.270
I never heard of another story of a man

00:39:59.270 --> 00:40:02.930
being sexually abused, whether
I was not allowing myself

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:05.624
to read it or see it, or
I didn't see stories on TV

00:40:05.624 --> 00:40:08.980
or I didn't see people, especially
athletes opening up about

00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:10.901
this publicly online.

00:40:10.901 --> 00:40:12.590
I just never saw it.

00:40:12.590 --> 00:40:15.570
I remember sitting down after
sharing this and for whatever

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:17.030
reason, right when I sat down,

00:40:17.030 --> 00:40:20.840
I just started erupting
with tears and emotion.

00:40:20.840 --> 00:40:22.700
And there was two women
on each side of me,

00:40:22.700 --> 00:40:26.240
who they're crying, they're hugging me.

00:40:26.240 --> 00:40:28.680
I'm ashamed, I'm embarrassed.

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:30.320
I run out of the room.

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:32.290
I run out of the room,

00:40:32.290 --> 00:40:34.770
out of the building in
this conference room

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:36.047
and I go outside and I'm like,

00:40:36.047 --> 00:40:37.217
"I'm not going back in there.

00:40:37.217 --> 00:40:39.247
"There's no way I could go
look at these people again.

00:40:39.247 --> 00:40:40.957
"Now that they know the worst part of me,

00:40:40.957 --> 00:40:42.287
"the thing I'm most ashamed of,

00:40:42.287 --> 00:40:44.767
"the thing I'm most embarrassed of,

00:40:44.767 --> 00:40:46.957
"the thing I feel the weakest about,

00:40:46.957 --> 00:40:49.187
"how would anyone ever actually accept me,

00:40:49.187 --> 00:40:51.367
"like me or love me after this moment?"

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:56.320
And probably one of the most
memorable and beautiful moments

00:40:56.320 --> 00:40:58.670
of my life was what happened next.

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:01.060
I felt a tap on my shoulder
a few minutes later,

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:03.620
I'm crying outside by myself,

00:41:03.620 --> 00:41:04.670
hand up against the wall.

00:41:04.670 --> 00:41:05.837
I'm like, "I don't want
anyone to look at me.

00:41:05.837 --> 00:41:07.581
"I'm so embarrassed."

00:41:07.581 --> 00:41:10.510
I feel a tap on my
shoulder and I turn around

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:14.010
and it's this big, big
guy, guy bigger than me,

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:16.410
older than me, probably
in his late fifties.

00:41:16.410 --> 00:41:17.580
He looks me straight in the eyes,

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:18.840
puts his hand around my shoulder.

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:21.167
And he said, "You're my hero.

00:41:21.167 --> 00:41:23.437
"This happened to me when I was 13.

00:41:23.437 --> 00:41:26.517
"My wife doesn't know,
I've got three kids.

00:41:26.517 --> 00:41:27.847
"No one knows.

00:41:27.847 --> 00:41:29.907
"I've never had the
courage to do what you just

00:41:29.907 --> 00:41:31.677
"did in this moment.

00:41:31.677 --> 00:41:35.087
"And it's, I felt like a
prisoner my entire life,

00:41:35.087 --> 00:41:36.877
"holding this in.

00:41:36.877 --> 00:41:40.410
"I'm gonna go tell my wife, thank you."

00:41:40.410 --> 00:41:43.451
One by one, the men from
the room all came out

00:41:43.451 --> 00:41:46.400
and they all shared stories with me.

00:41:46.400 --> 00:41:49.230
You know, a lot of the
men were sexually abused.

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:51.750
The men who weren't sexually
abused had something else.

00:41:51.750 --> 00:41:53.871
Some other challenge
that they were faced with

00:41:53.871 --> 00:41:55.320
that they were unwilling to share

00:41:55.320 --> 00:41:58.040
because they were
embarrassed by or ashamed.

00:41:58.040 --> 00:42:00.470
And that was a big wake up
call for me where I realized

00:42:00.470 --> 00:42:04.910
man, men in general, don't
have really a safe place

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:07.273
to share their shame.

00:42:08.130 --> 00:42:10.990
And it's what made me want
to go down a path of like

00:42:10.990 --> 00:42:13.960
researching, like how many
men has this happened to?

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:16.290
And I found one in six
men are sexually abused.

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:17.460
It's one in four for women.

00:42:17.460 --> 00:42:19.713
Obviously women face it a lot more.

00:42:20.620 --> 00:42:24.800
But as I started to write
my next book about this,

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:25.850
called the "Mask of Masculinity,"

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:28.887
I said, "You know, men, a
lot of the men in the world

00:42:28.887 --> 00:42:31.490
"are causing a lot of
the pain in the world."

00:42:31.490 --> 00:42:36.490
This is where you see some
of our leaders angry a lot.

00:42:36.600 --> 00:42:37.970
And, and in my mind, some of them,

00:42:37.970 --> 00:42:39.850
they're not looking for solutions to heal.

00:42:39.850 --> 00:42:43.050
They're looking to create
more harm, more war,

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:45.390
more fighting, more anger.

00:42:45.390 --> 00:42:47.687
And I said, "If men
started to heal their past,

00:42:47.687 --> 00:42:49.057
"they would be much more loving

00:42:49.057 --> 00:42:51.377
"and find better solutions in my opinion."

00:42:52.330 --> 00:42:54.990
And I remember going on tour about my book

00:42:54.990 --> 00:42:56.750
and I would get about 50/50 of the room,

00:42:56.750 --> 00:42:58.390
men and women show up.

00:42:58.390 --> 00:43:00.247
And I'd say, "Hey, just
show hands for the women.

00:43:00.247 --> 00:43:02.000
"How many of you women in the room

00:43:03.189 --> 00:43:04.437
"talk about your feelings?

00:43:04.437 --> 00:43:07.467
"You know, once a once
a week with girlfriends,

00:43:07.467 --> 00:43:09.137
"you talk about your marriage issues,

00:43:09.137 --> 00:43:10.577
"your body image issues,

00:43:10.577 --> 00:43:13.027
"your career, or whatever's
happening with your family.

00:43:13.027 --> 00:43:15.440
"You talk about your stuff."

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:17.380
All the women raised their
hand once a week, right?

00:43:17.380 --> 00:43:19.057
And I go, "Leave your hand up, women,

00:43:19.057 --> 00:43:20.427
"if you do this every day.

00:43:20.427 --> 00:43:22.057
"You're on the phone with a
girlfriend, you're at lunch,

00:43:22.057 --> 00:43:23.888
"you're having tea, whatever it is."

00:43:23.888 --> 00:43:25.294
And they're like, "Yep, this is every day

00:43:25.294 --> 00:43:26.127
"we talk about our stuff."

00:43:26.127 --> 00:43:27.670
And I go for the men in the room,

00:43:27.670 --> 00:43:29.130
couple hundred men in the room.

00:43:29.130 --> 00:43:32.137
I say, "Put your hand up
if you talk once a month

00:43:32.137 --> 00:43:35.487
"with one man about your marriage issues,

00:43:35.487 --> 00:43:38.467
"your body image issues,
your career stuff,

00:43:38.467 --> 00:43:41.280
"your insecurities, your
fears, your doubts."

00:43:41.280 --> 00:43:42.710
Maybe a couple of hands go up.

00:43:42.710 --> 00:43:44.357
And I go, "Are you guys
part of a church group

00:43:44.357 --> 00:43:47.177
"that does this once a month with 30 men

00:43:47.177 --> 00:43:48.760
"to talk about this in a safe space?"

00:43:48.760 --> 00:43:49.760
And they're like, "Yeah."

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:52.600
Pretty much everywhere I
went around the country.

00:43:52.600 --> 00:43:55.307
And I said, "Women who had your hands up,

00:43:55.307 --> 00:44:00.307
"imagine a world where you
never shared any of this ever,

00:44:00.507 --> 00:44:03.477
"where you kept it in a deep
place inside of your heart

00:44:03.477 --> 00:44:05.717
"and your soul, and you
never talked to a girlfriend

00:44:05.717 --> 00:44:07.307
"about this, you never
felt you could share

00:44:07.307 --> 00:44:09.717
"anything because you'd be shamed,

00:44:09.717 --> 00:44:11.687
"you'd be made fun of,
you'd be called names,

00:44:11.687 --> 00:44:14.327
"you'd be picked on, you'd
be punched in the face

00:44:14.327 --> 00:44:17.480
"maybe if you shared some of
these things, just imagine."

00:44:17.480 --> 00:44:19.880
Now I'm not saying it's
right what men have done

00:44:19.880 --> 00:44:22.510
or what they do in bad situations.

00:44:22.510 --> 00:44:24.400
I'm not saying any of
these things are justified,

00:44:24.400 --> 00:44:27.210
but just imagine the pain and
suffering that some men feel

00:44:27.210 --> 00:44:32.010
if they feel like they can
never share certain shames.

00:44:32.010 --> 00:44:35.530
And I want to create a world
where men can find a safe

00:44:35.530 --> 00:44:39.070
confidant or something where
they can share more frequently

00:44:39.070 --> 00:44:41.180
their pain, because I feel like that pain

00:44:41.180 --> 00:44:43.790
is causing pain in the world
when they don't share it.

00:44:43.790 --> 00:44:46.230
So it's been an interesting journey.

00:44:46.230 --> 00:44:47.980
- Well, it's so interesting
to hear you say that,

00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:50.320
because I think we touched
on this a little bit

00:44:50.320 --> 00:44:51.430
when I was on your show,

00:44:51.430 --> 00:44:54.450
but one of the things that
people would say about Jordan

00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:56.610
all the time, and especially
when we were on tour,

00:44:56.610 --> 00:44:59.860
they would say it's all angry young men

00:44:59.860 --> 00:45:01.250
who were showing up to his shows.

00:45:01.250 --> 00:45:04.390
Now, first off, that just
wasn't true demographically.

00:45:04.390 --> 00:45:07.740
It was usually about 60/40
to, you know, male to female,

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:09.540
but that's a pretty decent mix.

00:45:09.540 --> 00:45:12.431
And it was black people and white people

00:45:12.431 --> 00:45:13.890
and gay people and straight
people and all that.

00:45:13.890 --> 00:45:15.950
But I always thought
the criticism was so odd

00:45:15.950 --> 00:45:20.320
because even if it was a
hundred percent angry, white,

00:45:20.320 --> 00:45:21.700
that was the other one,
they were white too,

00:45:21.700 --> 00:45:22.533
which also wasn't true.

00:45:22.533 --> 00:45:24.592
But let's say it was a hundred

00:45:24.592 --> 00:45:26.370
percent angry, young white men.

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:29.240
Well, if his message was empowering them,

00:45:29.240 --> 00:45:31.110
if his message was freeing them,

00:45:31.110 --> 00:45:33.690
if his message was doing
actually everything that you just

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:36.830
described, well, then
that would be spectacular.

00:45:36.830 --> 00:45:39.700
And yet it was framed as if, if you talk,

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:43.410
even if you talk to these
people, just by these people

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:46.900
showing up to a place together
that that somehow is evil,

00:45:46.900 --> 00:45:48.910
which then just feeds the narrative

00:45:48.910 --> 00:45:51.180
that they've already got in their heads.

00:45:51.180 --> 00:45:53.170
- Yeah, I think for me,

00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:54.500
I think I talked about
this briefly with you.

00:45:54.500 --> 00:45:56.420
I don't know if it was
on our show or off camera

00:45:56.420 --> 00:46:00.390
that I remember during my
first book I think it was,

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:03.230
I was on Ellen and then
I was on Glenn Beck

00:46:03.230 --> 00:46:04.740
within a 24 hour window.

00:46:04.740 --> 00:46:05.780
And I remember people being like,

00:46:05.780 --> 00:46:08.070
how could you go on Glenn Beck?

00:46:08.070 --> 00:46:09.530
I think Glenn Beck's a nice guy.

00:46:09.530 --> 00:46:10.363
I like his stuff.

00:46:10.363 --> 00:46:12.580
But if you're one way and you're,

00:46:12.580 --> 00:46:13.810
you're not thinking that way,

00:46:13.810 --> 00:46:15.670
then there might be controversy.

00:46:15.670 --> 00:46:17.560
And I remember saying to myself, like,

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:19.910
just like Jordan Peterson,
his book's incredible.

00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:21.560
You know, he has great principles.

00:46:21.560 --> 00:46:26.170
I love his book and I'm
trying to just help humanity.

00:46:26.170 --> 00:46:27.630
I don't care what you believe,

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:28.700
what you think is right and wrong.

00:46:28.700 --> 00:46:31.640
I'm trying to lift people up
and give them tools to empower

00:46:31.640 --> 00:46:34.460
themselves, to live in peace,

00:46:34.460 --> 00:46:37.650
to live in power and to
inspire people around them.

00:46:37.650 --> 00:46:40.850
And I think that's our mission.

00:46:40.850 --> 00:46:43.770
- So I don't want to do
straight up politics with you.

00:46:43.770 --> 00:46:46.090
I did not get an essay book of the things

00:46:46.090 --> 00:46:47.400
that you won't talk about,

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:49.631
but I don't want to do politics with you,

00:46:49.631 --> 00:46:50.560
because I think it's a good escape.

00:46:50.560 --> 00:46:53.033
This is, this is meta politics in a way.

00:46:54.500 --> 00:46:56.750
But in a time like we live in right now

00:46:56.750 --> 00:46:58.740
where politics seems crazy,

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:00.470
everyone's trying to destroy each other,

00:47:00.470 --> 00:47:02.230
all of those things.

00:47:02.230 --> 00:47:05.450
Do you have any tricks that
you use for yourself or that

00:47:05.450 --> 00:47:08.490
you've recommended to
other people to scale back?

00:47:08.490 --> 00:47:10.430
I mean, one of the things
I try to show people

00:47:10.430 --> 00:47:13.900
is that your worldview
just can't be political.

00:47:13.900 --> 00:47:16.030
You know, if your
worldview is just politics,

00:47:16.030 --> 00:47:16.970
you're always doing this,

00:47:16.970 --> 00:47:19.060
you've got blinders on
and you need a much bigger

00:47:19.060 --> 00:47:22.130
philosophical, religious, whatever it is,

00:47:22.130 --> 00:47:24.550
whatever your world view is,
it needs to be much wider.

00:47:24.550 --> 00:47:25.460
Do you have any tricks?

00:47:25.460 --> 00:47:28.480
Because it seems like everyone everywhere.

00:47:28.480 --> 00:47:30.797
And I know I'm sort of self
selected into this thing,

00:47:30.797 --> 00:47:32.290
because when anyone sees me at the store,

00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:33.560
they want to talk politics with me.

00:47:33.560 --> 00:47:37.110
But, but I do sense it's
making people crazier.

00:47:37.110 --> 00:47:37.943
- Crazy, man.
- It's making

00:47:37.943 --> 00:47:39.390
people angrier.

00:47:39.390 --> 00:47:40.920
You know, they just
released this big study

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:43.310
about how depressed
people are right now and.

00:47:43.310 --> 00:47:48.310
- I remember when I started
being an entrepreneur

00:47:48.561 --> 00:47:51.220
after leaving my brother's,

00:47:51.220 --> 00:47:53.140
I paid 250 a month for my brother's place

00:47:53.140 --> 00:47:54.757
to have a room from his place, right?

00:47:54.757 --> 00:47:57.300
And I started making enough
money to get an apartment

00:47:57.300 --> 00:47:59.500
for 495 a month.

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:03.040
And it was the scariest thing
to write a check for 495.

00:48:03.040 --> 00:48:04.838
'Cause I was like, I
don't know how I'm gonna

00:48:04.838 --> 00:48:05.671
pay this next month.

00:48:05.671 --> 00:48:08.750
But I got the cheapest apartment
I could in Columbus, Ohio.

00:48:08.750 --> 00:48:10.776
And I remember, and I didn't
have a car at the time.

00:48:10.776 --> 00:48:11.609
So I was walking everywhere.

00:48:11.609 --> 00:48:12.477
And I remember saying to myself,

00:48:12.477 --> 00:48:14.380
"I'm not gonna get a TV."

00:48:14.380 --> 00:48:17.030
And for the first few
years I didn't have a TV.

00:48:17.030 --> 00:48:19.500
And I just focused on my
mission and my vision,

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:22.530
which was creation, which
was getting customers

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:26.550
and sales and adding value to
those customers and clients.

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:29.210
And not having a TV was a big blessing.

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:30.760
I love movies and I love TV.

00:48:30.760 --> 00:48:32.340
So I would go to the movies all the time,

00:48:32.340 --> 00:48:33.990
but I wouldn't allow myself to browse

00:48:33.990 --> 00:48:38.670
and see chaos on a screen until midnight

00:48:38.670 --> 00:48:42.030
and then go to sleep with
nightmares from the chaos.

00:48:42.030 --> 00:48:44.503
So personally, I tried to,

00:48:45.530 --> 00:48:48.450
I remember I had Marianne
Williamson on my show and I told

00:48:48.450 --> 00:48:50.397
her, you know, "I really
don't watch the news."

00:48:50.397 --> 00:48:52.237
And she goes, "That's, you
need to watch the news.

00:48:52.237 --> 00:48:53.717
"Like you need to be informed,

00:48:53.717 --> 00:48:55.027
"especially as like a white male,

00:48:55.027 --> 00:48:57.147
"you need to know what's
happening in the world."

00:48:57.147 --> 00:48:58.870
But you can get the news
from different places.

00:48:58.870 --> 00:49:02.130
I don't need to watch
the TV to get the news.

00:49:02.130 --> 00:49:04.710
I can check, you know,
Apple news or whatever,

00:49:04.710 --> 00:49:07.010
or get someone to tweet me
something and check it out.

00:49:07.010 --> 00:49:09.420
And people are gonna tell
you the news, no matter what.

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:10.777
- Right?

00:49:10.777 --> 00:49:11.610
You can't really escape the news.

00:49:11.610 --> 00:49:15.170
- I try to focus on, I truly
believe that when I focus

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:17.980
on giving to people and service,

00:49:17.980 --> 00:49:19.890
I'm a happier human being.

00:49:19.890 --> 00:49:23.230
And research has backed
science of happiness.

00:49:23.230 --> 00:49:25.160
That when you're in
contribution to someone else,

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:27.330
whether it's opening the
door, buying a coffee,

00:49:27.330 --> 00:49:29.750
having a conversation,
listening to someone

00:49:29.750 --> 00:49:31.490
when they're going through something,

00:49:31.490 --> 00:49:33.120
doing charity work, whatever it may be,

00:49:33.120 --> 00:49:34.180
that makes you the happiest.

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:35.440
When we're giving to others.

00:49:35.440 --> 00:49:37.200
So when we're focused on self,

00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:39.370
and what we don't have,

00:49:39.370 --> 00:49:43.430
suffering is the result of
obsessive, self-centric thinking.

00:49:43.430 --> 00:49:44.790
So when I'm thinking of the news,

00:49:44.790 --> 00:49:46.740
this is not working in my favor.

00:49:46.740 --> 00:49:49.390
I need to, this is an outrage,

00:49:49.390 --> 00:49:52.720
or I'm not happy about this,
'cause it's not helping me.

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:54.100
You're gonna be unhappy.

00:49:54.100 --> 00:49:56.537
And I'm not saying you shouldn't
fight for what you want

00:49:56.537 --> 00:49:58.480
and have things to change.

00:49:58.480 --> 00:49:59.313
Sure.

00:49:59.313 --> 00:50:02.240
But if you're gonna be,
if you want to be happier,

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:05.210
focus on other people, focus on service.

00:50:05.210 --> 00:50:07.970
And also don't watch the news.

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:09.950
And as media companies, ourselves,

00:50:09.950 --> 00:50:11.610
we want people to watch us.

00:50:11.610 --> 00:50:16.610
But I think watch, focus on
things that bring you joy.

00:50:17.230 --> 00:50:19.640
I think as adults, we don't play enough.

00:50:19.640 --> 00:50:23.280
You know, in my studio, I got a Frisbee

00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:24.950
and we love to work hard.

00:50:24.950 --> 00:50:26.280
You know, our team works hard,

00:50:26.280 --> 00:50:29.287
but I'll grab Matt, our
COO, my friend, and I say,

00:50:29.287 --> 00:50:31.410
"We need five minutes just
to throw the Frisbee."

00:50:31.410 --> 00:50:32.800
We need to play.

00:50:32.800 --> 00:50:34.200
Who plays anymore?
- I know.

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:36.210
- Especially if you can't go
outside, you can't do this.

00:50:36.210 --> 00:50:37.360
Who's playing?

00:50:37.360 --> 00:50:38.730
- Well, I'm chasing a one year old dog.

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:40.030
- Exactly, right?

00:50:40.030 --> 00:50:44.773
But I'm like a big advocate
of play over watching TV.

00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:47.660
Go do something fun.

00:50:47.660 --> 00:50:50.520
I went out to, was it, I've
been in LA for eight years.

00:50:50.520 --> 00:50:51.850
How long have you been here?

00:50:51.850 --> 00:50:52.990
- About eight years.
- Eight years.

00:50:52.990 --> 00:50:54.639
- Yeah, Seven and a half.

00:50:54.639 --> 00:50:55.472
- Literally, two days ago,

00:50:55.472 --> 00:50:59.480
The first time I went to
Franklin Canyon Lake or park,

00:50:59.480 --> 00:51:00.410
have you been to this place?

00:51:00.410 --> 00:51:01.970
- I don't even know what that is.

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:03.830
- This is 10 minutes from here.

00:51:03.830 --> 00:51:07.750
It's a lake and nature of like
a hundred acres in the middle

00:51:07.750 --> 00:51:09.060
of LA and you can't see the city.

00:51:09.060 --> 00:51:11.920
It's literally in the
middle of Mullholland Drive.

00:51:11.920 --> 00:51:13.920
- Never heard of this place.
- It's unbelievable.

00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:15.636
I saw a photo of it on Instagram.

00:51:15.636 --> 00:51:16.640
- We got a lake?

00:51:16.640 --> 00:51:17.910
- We got a lake and it's like ducks

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:19.613
and turtles and everything.

00:51:20.833 --> 00:51:22.420
And I went there on Sunday
with my girlfriend and our dog.

00:51:22.420 --> 00:51:23.840
And it was just like, we're in nature.

00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:25.499
We're hanging out.

00:51:25.499 --> 00:51:26.497
I'm not watching TV.

00:51:26.497 --> 00:51:27.330
I'm not on social media.

00:51:27.330 --> 00:51:29.070
I'm a big salsa dancer.

00:51:29.070 --> 00:51:30.240
I love salsa.
- Really?

00:51:30.240 --> 00:51:33.350
- For 15 years I've been salsa
dancing around the world.

00:51:33.350 --> 00:51:34.560
I play guitar.

00:51:34.560 --> 00:51:36.770
I like to play sports, workout.

00:51:36.770 --> 00:51:39.410
I'm just like, go do something
that's gonna contribute

00:51:39.410 --> 00:51:42.200
and benefit your life and
contribute and benefit

00:51:42.200 --> 00:51:43.410
someone else's life.

00:51:43.410 --> 00:51:46.460
When you do those things,
you're gonna be a better person.

00:51:46.460 --> 00:51:47.350
I'm learning Spanish.

00:51:47.350 --> 00:51:49.310
I'm trying all these different
things that get me away

00:51:49.310 --> 00:51:52.110
from the train wreck of the news.

00:51:52.110 --> 00:51:53.080
So that's what I recommend.

00:51:53.080 --> 00:51:55.670
Don't obsess yourself into
what's not working for you.

00:51:55.670 --> 00:51:56.710
Go be the change.

00:51:56.710 --> 00:51:59.790
- Do you sense that right
now because of corona,

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:03.820
because of protests, because
everything is so seemingly

00:52:03.820 --> 00:52:07.250
chaotic, that people are
becoming more receptive

00:52:07.250 --> 00:52:08.780
to the type of stuff you're talking about,

00:52:08.780 --> 00:52:10.960
because I can just, for me,

00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:13.610
like I started gardening
since we started doing this.

00:52:13.610 --> 00:52:14.680
I'll show you when we get out here,

00:52:14.680 --> 00:52:16.120
we got a ton of, we're growing a ton

00:52:16.120 --> 00:52:17.640
of great stuff out there.
- Grow some carrots, man.

00:52:17.640 --> 00:52:19.610
- I got, real carrots, I got tomatoes,

00:52:19.610 --> 00:52:21.070
I got cucumbers, I got zucchini.

00:52:21.070 --> 00:52:23.470
I got broccoli, peppers.

00:52:23.470 --> 00:52:24.930
But like I started doing that.

00:52:24.930 --> 00:52:25.810
I'm cooking a lot more.

00:52:25.810 --> 00:52:26.950
David usually cooks a ton,

00:52:26.950 --> 00:52:28.510
but now I'm barbecuing all the time.

00:52:28.510 --> 00:52:30.240
And like I'm refining some skills.

00:52:30.240 --> 00:52:33.180
And like, I think there's
a unique opportunity

00:52:33.180 --> 00:52:36.170
in this mess to either come out of it,

00:52:36.170 --> 00:52:38.340
more skilled, more refined,

00:52:38.340 --> 00:52:40.230
more passionate about what you do,

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:42.440
or maybe realize that
what you were doing before

00:52:42.440 --> 00:52:44.550
isn't what you want to
do or all that stuff.

00:52:44.550 --> 00:52:47.420
Versus some people that I think
are just gonna have slogged

00:52:47.420 --> 00:52:48.760
through it and not really thought

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:50.870
about their life critically enough.

00:52:50.870 --> 00:52:52.210
And then they'll just be on the other side

00:52:52.210 --> 00:52:53.955
and the world'll be different.

00:52:53.955 --> 00:52:54.993
- I'm on both sides here.

00:52:56.080 --> 00:53:00.080
If you need to take a pause
and rest for these last three

00:53:00.080 --> 00:53:02.380
months, because you've
been, go, go, go, go,

00:53:02.380 --> 00:53:05.280
nonstop for 10 years, and
you had never taken a pause

00:53:05.280 --> 00:53:07.340
in your life, this is
the most beautiful time

00:53:07.340 --> 00:53:09.030
for you to sit around and do nothing

00:53:09.030 --> 00:53:11.680
and read a book and lounge and not hustle.

00:53:11.680 --> 00:53:14.633
So for some people, I think
that is what you need.

00:53:15.821 --> 00:53:17.750
And personally, my
philosophy is I feel like

00:53:17.750 --> 00:53:19.600
this is what the world needed in general,

00:53:19.600 --> 00:53:22.740
is for us to take a pause
with technology being so fast,

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:24.440
with us on social media.

00:53:24.440 --> 00:53:25.990
Most people are on
their phones six, seven,

00:53:25.990 --> 00:53:27.050
eight hours a day.

00:53:27.050 --> 00:53:28.220
And a lot of it's social media.

00:53:28.220 --> 00:53:31.630
It's like, what are we
consuming our time with?

00:53:31.630 --> 00:53:34.263
It's not what we're supposed
to be doing all day.

00:53:34.263 --> 00:53:35.250
- Pretty sure it's not making us happier.

00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:36.160
- It's not making us happier.

00:53:36.160 --> 00:53:37.750
It's making us more neurotic of like,

00:53:37.750 --> 00:53:39.010
I need more likes, I need more followers,

00:53:39.010 --> 00:53:40.350
I need to, oh, what's
happening in the news?

00:53:40.350 --> 00:53:42.800
It's just, it's craziness.

00:53:42.800 --> 00:53:45.610
And so for some, I
think don't do anything.

00:53:45.610 --> 00:53:46.890
And that's what you need.

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:49.930
You need to reevaluate why
your life isn't working,

00:53:49.930 --> 00:53:51.460
why this relation, I'm
seeing so many people.

00:53:51.460 --> 00:53:52.860
I'm not sure if you're seeing this,

00:53:52.860 --> 00:53:54.767
a lot of big personalities are posting,

00:53:54.767 --> 00:53:56.687
"We're getting a divorce.

00:53:56.687 --> 00:53:58.160
"We're going through a breakup."

00:53:58.160 --> 00:54:00.407
You're finally evaluating
your relationship.

00:54:00.407 --> 00:54:03.310
- You're trapped in a house
with someone every minute

00:54:03.310 --> 00:54:04.687
for three months and
you're constantly going,

00:54:04.687 --> 00:54:06.860
"Do I like this guy?"
- You can't escape anymore.

00:54:06.860 --> 00:54:09.480
So I think people are getting
clear on what they want.

00:54:09.480 --> 00:54:11.160
'Cause they haven't been
clear for a long time,

00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:13.010
maybe partly, but not fully.

00:54:13.010 --> 00:54:15.010
So for some people you need a pause.

00:54:15.010 --> 00:54:15.843
You need a break.

00:54:15.843 --> 00:54:17.890
You need to evaluate, you
need to take inventory

00:54:17.890 --> 00:54:19.350
of everything in your life

00:54:19.350 --> 00:54:21.740
and create a new game plan moving forward.

00:54:21.740 --> 00:54:23.340
You need to find some coaches and mentors

00:54:23.340 --> 00:54:25.420
and have a game plan and go take action.

00:54:25.420 --> 00:54:28.410
For other people, I'm
kind of in the middle.

00:54:28.410 --> 00:54:31.660
I feel like I've hustled
more, my business,

00:54:31.660 --> 00:54:32.890
I've hired more people.

00:54:32.890 --> 00:54:35.070
I've been like more creative than ever,

00:54:35.070 --> 00:54:36.790
but I'm also going to the park

00:54:36.790 --> 00:54:39.100
and relaxing with my girlfriend
and my dog on the weekends,

00:54:39.100 --> 00:54:40.770
which I would never do.

00:54:40.770 --> 00:54:43.040
And I'm taking that time to be in nature.

00:54:43.040 --> 00:54:45.180
So I feel like I'm trying to
do both and balance my personal

00:54:45.180 --> 00:54:47.110
life, but then work going kind of all

00:54:47.110 --> 00:54:48.380
in and getting clear on like,

00:54:48.380 --> 00:54:49.880
what does our audience need right now?

00:54:49.880 --> 00:54:51.240
What does our customers need?

00:54:51.240 --> 00:54:52.890
What do they need for the
next five to 10 years,

00:54:52.890 --> 00:54:54.600
if this doesn't change?

00:54:54.600 --> 00:54:57.000
So I'm a little in the middle
and I have empathy for both

00:54:57.000 --> 00:54:59.640
sides, but I think if you can rediscover

00:54:59.640 --> 00:55:02.110
what you want to do in your
life and who you want to become,

00:55:02.110 --> 00:55:05.110
whether you take action on it
now or just discover it now,

00:55:05.110 --> 00:55:06.200
that's what we should be doing.

00:55:06.200 --> 00:55:07.860
- How do you decide how much stuff

00:55:07.860 --> 00:55:09.750
to put out there in the midst of all this?

00:55:09.750 --> 00:55:11.860
'Cause a lot of what you're
talking about is to ease

00:55:11.860 --> 00:55:13.250
up on this stuff.

00:55:13.250 --> 00:55:15.550
Go live your life, get
out there in nature,

00:55:15.550 --> 00:55:16.480
et cetera, et cetera.

00:55:16.480 --> 00:55:19.300
I think I mentioned to you last
time, like people say to me,

00:55:19.300 --> 00:55:21.170
Dave, why don't you do
an interview every day?

00:55:21.170 --> 00:55:22.680
Do five interviews a week,

00:55:22.680 --> 00:55:24.760
do seven interviews a
week and we do one or two.

00:55:24.760 --> 00:55:26.560
And then I obviously do some other stuff.

00:55:26.560 --> 00:55:28.670
And I, it's not for a lack of time,

00:55:28.670 --> 00:55:32.620
but it really in many reasons
for a respect of my audience.

00:55:32.620 --> 00:55:34.400
- Of people's time.
- Yeah, like it's,

00:55:34.400 --> 00:55:36.160
'cause I, it's like if you listen to me,

00:55:36.160 --> 00:55:38.090
then hopefully you listened
to one of your episodes.

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:41.626
Maybe you listened to Sam
Harris or Joe Rogan or whatever.

00:55:41.626 --> 00:55:43.130
It's like, I want you to, that's great.

00:55:43.130 --> 00:55:44.970
If you give a couple hours
of your week to this stuff,

00:55:44.970 --> 00:55:47.920
that's fantastic, but I
don't want you to give

00:55:47.920 --> 00:55:49.500
40 hours of your week.

00:55:49.500 --> 00:55:51.160
So I don't even want you to give 20 hours

00:55:51.160 --> 00:55:52.871
of your week to this.

00:55:52.871 --> 00:55:54.350
So like, I think it's
important what we're doing,

00:55:54.350 --> 00:55:55.767
it's valuable what we're doing,

00:55:55.767 --> 00:55:58.570
but how do you decide like what's enough

00:55:58.570 --> 00:56:00.530
and what's too much?

00:56:00.530 --> 00:56:01.363
- Three factors.

00:56:01.363 --> 00:56:03.330
One is like, what is my audience?

00:56:03.330 --> 00:56:05.720
What I think my audience
wants and my intuitive side

00:56:05.720 --> 00:56:07.920
of what I think they
are gonna want or need.

00:56:08.790 --> 00:56:10.450
What works for me personally.

00:56:10.450 --> 00:56:11.920
Like I don't want to be on every day

00:56:11.920 --> 00:56:13.624
doing an interview either.

00:56:13.624 --> 00:56:14.640
It's a lot of energy.

00:56:14.640 --> 00:56:16.510
What personally works for
my life, my lifestyle,

00:56:16.510 --> 00:56:17.840
my vision for the future.

00:56:17.840 --> 00:56:19.200
And then what does the data say?

00:56:19.200 --> 00:56:21.000
And I know you're
probably very data driven

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:23.270
as well with your team.
- You have to be.

00:56:23.270 --> 00:56:24.630
- Gotta be if you're
in the media business.

00:56:24.630 --> 00:56:28.160
So we're looking at how can
we create content on certain

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:30.780
platforms more and less
on other platforms,

00:56:30.780 --> 00:56:32.040
based on the data?

00:56:32.040 --> 00:56:33.290
So I'm kind of thinking
of those three things,

00:56:33.290 --> 00:56:35.370
my personal energy and time,

00:56:35.370 --> 00:56:37.330
intuitively what I
think my audience needs.

00:56:37.330 --> 00:56:38.610
And what's the data saying?

00:56:38.610 --> 00:56:40.660
And we're making decisions based on that.

00:56:41.616 --> 00:56:43.270
- I think we did it all here.

00:56:43.270 --> 00:56:44.594
Well, you know what?

00:56:44.594 --> 00:56:45.610
I got one more for you.

00:56:45.610 --> 00:56:47.360
I'm gonna loosely quote a
pretty decent interviewer.

00:56:47.360 --> 00:56:49.620
What is greatness to you?

00:56:49.620 --> 00:56:52.637
- Greatness to me, it has
changed over the years

00:56:52.637 --> 00:56:54.800
and I think it will
always evolve and change

00:56:54.800 --> 00:56:58.253
until we find kind of our
true purpose and mission.

00:57:01.210 --> 00:57:02.510
For me currently in the moment,

00:57:02.510 --> 00:57:05.650
it's discovering your unique
talents and gifts that you were

00:57:05.650 --> 00:57:08.830
born with, developing those in the pursuit

00:57:08.830 --> 00:57:11.380
of your dreams and in that pursuit,

00:57:11.380 --> 00:57:13.840
making an impact on the people around you.

00:57:13.840 --> 00:57:17.760
I think we do that, we
figure out who we are,

00:57:17.760 --> 00:57:20.260
what our talents are and cultivate them.

00:57:20.260 --> 00:57:22.310
We go pursue the thing
that brings us a lot of joy

00:57:22.310 --> 00:57:25.290
and we make other people
happy and impacted around us,

00:57:25.290 --> 00:57:26.123
in a positive way.

00:57:26.123 --> 00:57:27.900
I feel like that's greatness.

00:57:27.900 --> 00:57:29.340
- Plus we're in a great jacket, man.

00:57:29.340 --> 00:57:30.940
- It's clean.

00:57:30.940 --> 00:57:32.572
You have buyer's remorse now.

00:57:32.572 --> 00:57:33.749
(laughing)

00:57:33.749 --> 00:57:35.150
- It's @lewishowes on Twitter.

